# Who stands behind each claim, and on what authority. One row per three-point claim in every
# loaded document: the party asserting it, the standing they claim, and the typed reason where
# they claim none.
#
# What separates an auditor from a hunch
#   `Dependence` describes `standing` as "the axis separating a statutory auditor's observation from
#   a controller's hunch". Ingest reads it into columns so it can be asked at all, and
#   tests/state_coverage.rs gives every state a document can file a verdict, so an element ingest
#   keeps no column for is declared, never lost in silence.
#
# Almost nobody claims any
#   The first thing this relation says is that stated standings are rare, beside explicit refusals
#   and claims carrying no provenance element at all. `examples/observations` prints the three
#   figures.
#   That is a finding about the evidence, not a defect in it: `unmeasured` here reads "no standing
#   vocabulary is published for this assertion", which is the honest answer when no authority
#   publishes a list to borrow from.
#
# The three states are not two
#   A claim with no `provenance` element said nothing; one whose `standing` is `absent` was asked
#   and answered. Merging them would count a silence as a refusal, the same collapse
#   `StatedBorrowedTerm`'s own annotation refuses at `Regime/framework`.
-- pm:Claim/pm:provenance, with pm:StatedBorrowedTerm at pm:standing.
SELECT c.filing, c.seq, c.owns, c.layer,
       c.prov_party AS party,
       c.prov_standing_taxonomy AS taxonomy,
       c.prov_standing_value    AS standing,
       c.prov_standing_absent   AS standing_absent,
       CASE WHEN c.prov_standing_value  IS NOT NULL THEN 'claimed'
            WHEN c.prov_standing_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'asked and refused'
            ELSE 'no provenance filed' END AS what_the_claim_says
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c
